    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah i'll keep a close eye on her... it just feels like something's hiding in there. like, even if she looks okay on the surface, what if there's like a little something small and nasty growing or causing trouble deep down where i can't see it? is it common for these kinds of infections to just sort of \"sneak up\" on you?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, it is surprisingly common for intra-abdominal infections to progress insidiously before becoming visible externally, especially if the initial blockage allowed material to lodge deep in the rumen wall. These \"silent\" infections can develop rapidly over a few days if the aspiration void or foreign material remains undetected, which is why monitoring her temperament and appetite is just as critical as watching the incision itself. Please consult a veterinarian immediately if you notice a slight rise in temperature, reduced appetite, or just general unease, as these are often the earliest warnings of a developing abscess or leak.",
